{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_10-42-39-etcd_raft-real-run/draft && grep -n \\\"defect_claim\\\\|rationale\\\" update-3.json | head\"","aggregated_output":"2:  \"rationale\": \"The successor experiment executed in a clean copy (a9f71d7a07454d058d818148f7479e8f, completed, exit 0) and reproduced the witness with every comparison complete and evaluated: the re-added voter's invocation did not return within the window, released only on the caller's own deadline, and its payload was never committed, while the never-removed member and the same node after a leader change returned immediately and committed. This update attributes that retained interpretation, adds the snapshot's own node-level expectation about when proposal blocking is intended as a source anchor, and records the resulting knowledge. No new experiment is submitted: the open discriminator is answered and an unchanged repeat would add no evidence.\",\n26:      \"defect_claim\": \"asserted\",\n27:      \"rationale\": \"Two retained executions of the same fixed scenario agree. In a9f71d7a07454d058d818148f7479e8f (successor harness, distinct per-operation event names) all seven comparisons are complete and evaluated as holds: the re-admission of node 3 was applied on node 3 (index 4: change add, ok true, voter true, leader 1, term 1), the node context recorded node 3 as a voter with leader 1 (index 5), the invocation was admitted (index 6: phase after_readmission, leader 1, deadline 2500 ms), it had not returned after 1200 ms (index 7: returned false, err empty, elapsed_ms 1200), it released only when the caller's deadline expired (index 8: err 'context deadline exceeded', elapsed_ms 2501), and its payload appeared in no node's committed entries (index 9: present false, observed_nodes null). The control on never-removed node 2 with the same leader 1 returned in 0 ms (index 11) and was committed on all three nodes (index 12); after the forced campaign produced leader 2 at term 2 (index 13) the same node 3 invocation returned in 0 ms (index 15) and was committed on all three nodes (index 16). The earlier receipt 43a04622beef40588679c6cf1c40d6ad shows the identical values at the corresponding indices (witness 7/8/9, control 11/12, repair 15/16), so the difference is a property of the scenario, not of one run. The source account identifies the mechanism: the run loop's proposal gate is assigned only at node.go:374, 377 and 426 and read for proposals at node.go:525; re-admission cannot enter the removal guard (node.go:401-412) because the local Progress entry is already gone, so MsgProp is offered to a nil channel and can end only via the caller's context or node stop. The snapshot's own node test (node_test.go:352-385) scopes intended blocking to a node that does not know the current leader and expects the invocation to be accepted once a leader is known, which is precisely the condition the re-added voter satisfies while still being blocked; no test in the snapshot removes and re-adds a member through the Node API. The control also excludes the rival explanations that the cluster or leader was stalled (index 12) or that node 3's own state machine could not forward proposals (index 16).\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
